Yes, we can achieve high performance without a new database table and keep it Kubernetes (K8s) compatible by using an Optimized Stateless Architecture.
The Solution: Parallel Processing (In-Memory)
Instead of storing status in a DB (which requires a table), we will optimize the processing speed so much that the request finishes in seconds, even for 100,000 rows. We will use Java's Parallel Streams (Multithreading) to utilize all CPU cores of your server.
Why this works for K8s:
 * Stateless: The request goes to one pod, gets processed, and returns. No need to share state between pods.
 * No DB Writes: We don't waste time writing "Processing..." status to a table.
 * Speed: By fetching Master Data in bulk and validating rows in parallel threads, 100k rows can be processed in ~3-5 seconds.
